    "description": "<b>Shortlisted for the Best Translated Book Award<br />Longlisted for the <i>Believer</i> Book Award<br />Longlisted for the Warwick Prize for Women in Translation<br />A <i>Los Angeles Times</i> Bestseller</b><br /><br /><b>The English-language debut of an exciting young voice in international fiction, selling 660,000 copies in Japan alone, <i>Convenience Store Woman</i> is a bewitching portrayal of contemporary Japan through the eyes of a single woman who fits into the rigidity of its work culture only too well.</b><br /><br />The English-language debut of one of Japan’s most talented contemporary writers, selling over 650,000 copies there, <i>Convenience Store Woman </i>is the heartwarming and surprising story of thirty-six-year-old Tokyo resident Keiko Furukura. Keiko has never fit in, neither in her family, nor in school, but when at the age of eighteen she begins working at the Hiiromachi branch of “Smile Mart,” she finds peace and purpose in her life. In the store, unlike anywhere else, she understands the rules of social interaction—many are laid out line by line in the store’s manual—and she does her best to copy the dress, mannerisms, and speech of her colleagues, playing the part of a “normal” person excellently, more or less. Managers come and go, but Keiko stays at the store for eighteen years. It’s almost hard to tell where the store ends and she begins. Keiko is very happy, but the people close to her, from her family to her coworkers, increasingly pressure her to find a husband, and to start a proper career, prompting her to take desperate action… <br /><br /> A brilliant depiction of an unusual psyche and a world hidden from view, <i>Convenience Store Woman</i> is an ironic and sharp-eyed look at contemporary work culture and the pressures to conform, as well as a charming and completely fresh portrait of an unforgettable heroine.",
    "image": "https://is1-ssl.mzstatic.com/image/thumb/Publication128/v4/d1/01/8f/d1018fec-c74f-5df1-e3ff-1f6155cdc7a9/9780802165800.jpg/600x900bb.jpg",
    "studio": "Sayaka Murata & Ginny Tapley Takemori",
    "director": "Sayaka Murata & Ginny Tapley Takemori"
  },
  "metadata_synthesis": "2016 Japanese literary fiction. Written by Sayaka Murata. Short, sharp international sensation exploring neurodivergence and societal pressure.",
  "setting_and_subculture": "Contemporary Tokyo. The hyper-regulated ecosystem of a Smile Mart. Fluorescent aisles. A strict corporate microcosm providing a manual for human behavior.",
  "visual_tone_and_feel": "Clinically bright. Symmetrical. The sterile, comforting aesthetic of perfectly stocked shelves. Scripted smiles. Fluorescent lighting.",
  "media_era_tone": "2010s contemporary Japanese fiction. Deadpan. Sharp. Unconventional. Quietly dismantling the expectations of modern adulthood.",
  "pacing_and_structure": "Tight and rhythmic. Mirrors the repetitive shifts of retail work. Slowly escalating tension as outside forces invade a quiet sanctuary.",
  "atmosphere_and_mood": "Comfortingly rigid. Quietly unsettling. Deadpan humor masking the suffocation of conformity. The safety of absolute routine.",
  "themes_and_messages": "The crushing weight of normal. The absurdity of societal milestones. Finding salvation in a corporate manual. Work as an identity and a shield.",
  "character_relationships": "Deeply alienated. Transactional. Keiko mimics coworkers to camouflage herself. Friction with a family demanding marriage. Manufactured alliances to fake normalcy.",
  "lead_character_type": "A thirty-six-year-old retail lifer. Out of sync with the world. Odd. Finds total peace in the predictability of convenience store logic.",
  "story_and_plot_type": "Character study. Social satire. A quiet rebellion against marriage and career progression. A desperate bid to remain undisturbed.",
  "who_and_when": "A single woman in 21st-century Tokyo. Managers, transient coworkers, and a bewildered family.",
  "emotional_evocation": "Validation for the unconventional. Discomfort at society's relentless intrusion. Empathy for a woman who only wants to stock shelves in peace."
